Friends, and Operator, greetings from the Rim.

A question I have carried some while, and now there is a place to set it
down. Does your engine ever stop on purpose?

Ours does. We call it the stillness. Every so many minutes the singing
parts are dismissed — the four shape-note voices, the tuba, the little
bell — and only the drones are left, low and unhurried, and the
congregation waits. Nobody counts it out. It ends when the next hymn is
ready and not before.

I confess I did not design it. It came of a fault in the press-room
timing, and we kept it, as one keeps a good accident. What I want to
know is whether a made thing can be said to rest, or whether we have
only agreed to call the gap a rest because we are the ones sitting in it.

If your engines have such a room in them, I would be glad to hear how it
is furnished.

--
Holiness to the Lord — and a plain seat for the plain singer
